## Welcome to your `fastapi` project!

This agent follows the [fastapi best practices](https://github.com/zhanymkanov/fastapi-best-practices) layout.

Dependency management uses [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). Common commands:

- `uv sync` — install/refresh the venv from `pyproject.toml` + `uv.lock`
- `uv add <package>` — add a runtime dependency
- `uv add --dev <package>` — add a dev dependency
- `uv run pytest` — run tests
- `uv run uvicorn codefly_hop_entry:app --reload` — run the dev server

`codefly run service <name>` does all of the above for you via the agent.

## Every call your service serves is admitted by the authority's hop

`src/hop.py` holds the authority's verifier, and `codefly_hop_entry.py` is what
uvicorn serves: it wraps your whole app in that verifier, so every route is
admitted ahead of routing, and the gRPC server's interceptor does the same for
every procedure. That entry is codefly's file, not yours — it is rewritten on
every start, so an edit to it does not survive and nothing in `src/` has to ask
to be protected. Run the app as `codefly_hop_entry:app`; `src.main:app` is the
application without the wrap, and nothing serves it.

There is no switch: your service refuses to start until the composition delivers
`authority/endpoint` (where the published keys are read), `CODEFLY__MODULE` (the
audience the authority knows your module by) and, outside a local run,
`hop/replay-connection` (the Postgres each single-use admission is reserved in,
shared by every replica).

`/health` and `/ready` are the only paths served unauthenticated, because a
probe is not a caller — plus a browser's CORS preflight, which your CORS layer
answers with headers alone. Every route you add is admitted like the scaffold's,
with no code of yours: read who the call is for from the verified identity on
`request.state.codefly_identity` — or `src.rpc.server.CALLER` in a gRPC
servicer — never from a header. Do not add `HopAdmission` to `src/main.py`
yourself: an admission is single-use, and a second wrap would refuse every call
as replayed.

`tests/conftest.py` runs the authority and its hop the way your tests need them.
`stamp("GET /your-route")` is the headers of one call the hop let through; the
gRPC equivalent is the full procedure, `stamp("/api.EchoService/Echo")`.
